\section{The codes in \code{administered_by} and \code{claimed_by}}{

Mostly ISO 3166-1 alpha-3, so they join the \code{iso3c} spine directly -- but not
entirely, and the exceptions are the point of the table. Six parties here are
entities ISO assigns no code to, and they are written with a mnemonic
placeholder instead: \code{ABK} (Abkhazia), \code{CYP-N} (Northern Cyprus), \code{OST}
(South Ossetia), \code{PMR} (Transnistria), \code{SAH} (the Sahrawi Arab Democratic
Republic) and \code{SOL} (Somaliland).

Five of them -- \code{ABK}, \code{CYP-N}, \code{OST}, \code{PMR} and \code{SOL} -- appear in
\code{administered_by} for the like-named territory, which has no ISO code of its
own: the entity administers itself and ISO codes neither. \code{SAH} is the
exception and worth knowing about: it appears only as a \emph{claimant}, of
Western Sahara, which ISO does code (\code{ESH}) and which \code{MAR} administers.

None of the six are ISO codes, so none will resolve through
\code{\link[=convert_country]{convert_country()}} or any other \code{iso3c} lookup. Filter them out with
\verb{\%in\% country_codes()$iso3c} if you need a strictly ISO-keyed column.
